from pathlib import Path
import html,textwrap
ROOT=Path.cwd()
CASES=[
('short-url','Short URL','A college shares a long event-registration URL using /aB72.', 'Create links, redirect active links, expire/disable abuse. Redirect reads should stay fast; losing the mapping is worse than losing a click metric.', 'POST /links {targetUrl}; GET /{code}. Link(code unique, target, owner, expires_at, status). Reject unsafe URL schemes.', ['Browser','Redirect API','Mapping cache','Link database','Click event queue','Analytics worker'], [(0,1,'GET /aB72'),(1,2,'lookup code'),(1,3,'miss: read mapping'),(1,4,'async click'),(4,5,'aggregate')], 'Create validates and persists a unique mapping. Redirect checks cache, then database only on miss, verifies active status and returns 302 Location. The browser follows that Location; the API does not download the destination page. Analytics runs after the critical decision.', 'Cache descriptions/mappings according to an explicit freshness policy. Keep mutable redirects from being permanently cached. Use unique insertion and collision retries for codes.', 'Redis outage must not unleash unlimited fallback reads. Apply admission control. Define takedown propagation targets across caches.', '100M redirects/day ÷ 86,400 = about 1,157/second average; assume a separate peak factor, then estimate cache-miss load. These are illustrative numbers.', 'What happens if a mapping is disabled while its cached redirect is still fresh?'),
('whatsapp-style-messaging','WhatsApp-style messaging','Alice sends Bob a message while he is offline.', 'Durable send, history, reconnect, per-conversation ordering and separate accepted/delivered/read states. This is an illustrative chat architecture, not WhatsApp internals.', 'POST /conversations/{id}/messages; GET messages?after=cursor. Message(conversation, sequence, sender, client_message_id unique within its scope, ciphertext). DeviceCursor tracks delivery.', ['Alice device','Message API / sockets','Durable message DB','Delivery event queue','Delivery worker','Bob devices'],[(0,1,'stable message ID'),(1,2,'persist before ack'),(1,3,'committed outbox event'),(3,4,'retry delivery'),(4,5,'push when online')], 'Authenticate membership, deduplicate the send and commit message plus outbox. Acknowledge acceptance only after the durability contract is met. Worker delivers to connected devices. Bob fetches from durable history after reconnect even if a push notification was lost.', 'Use conversation sequences rather than phone clocks. Consumers deduplicate IDs. For end-to-end encryption, encrypt on the sender and decrypt on recipient devices with an established protocol.', 'If the worker crashes after push but before recording progress, retry can duplicate delivery. Devices deduplicate. Offline users cannot rely on socket memory.', '100,000 connected users sending one message/minute produce about 1,667 sends/second before group fan-out and multi-device copies.', 'How does Bob recover if he receives sequence 42 before sequence 41?'),
('e-commerce-checkout','E-commerce checkout','Two buyers request the last mug; one payment response is lost.', 'Create one order per purchase, prevent overselling, reconcile payment uncertainty and fulfill only confirmed orders.', 'POST /checkout with idempotency key. Order, OrderLine, Reservation(state, expiry), PaymentAttempt(provider key/status), Outbox. Calculate price on the server.', ['Buyer','Checkout API','Orders + stock DB','Payment work queue','Payment worker','Payment provider'],[(0,1,'stable purchase key'),(1,2,'reserve + outbox TX'),(1,3,'via outbox relay'),(3,4,'process request'),(4,5,'stable provider key')], 'Reserve stock with a conditional update and commit pending order plus outbox in one local transaction. The relay publishes payment work. A worker calls the provider outside that transaction. Verified callbacks/reconciliation update guarded order state, then schedule fulfillment.', 'The provider charge and local database commit are not one ACID transaction. A saga persists progress and compensates with stock release/refund when required.', 'Treat a charge timeout as unknown. Query/retry with the same provider key. Define policy for late success after reservation expiry; never blindly release or fulfill twice.', 'For 60,000 checkouts/hour, average is 16.7/second. Flash-sale concentration on a single SKU may matter more than total average.', 'Which local records must commit together to stop an order existing without a recoverable payment request?'),
('uber-style-ride-hailing','Uber-style ride hailing','A rider at a railway station needs an available driver who can reach them soon.', 'Location freshness, nearby search, one driver per active assignment and durable trip lifecycle. Illustrative design, not Uber internals.', 'POST /rides; POST /offers/{id}/accept with expected state/version. DriverPosition(timestamp, coordinates), Trip(state, rider, driver, version), Assignment.', ['Rider app','Matching service','Geographic index','Assignment / trip DB','Offer delivery','Driver app'],[(0,1,'request pickup'),(1,2,'fresh candidates'),(1,3,'atomic assignment'),(1,4,'expiring offer'),(4,5,'driver acceptance')], 'Find drivers in nearby cells including neighbors, discard stale positions, rank a shortlist by road ETA and send expiring offers. On acceptance, atomically claim available driver and pending trip within the authoritative boundary. Only committed assignment is confirmed to clients.', 'Geographic index is a candidate view, not proof of availability. Use guarded state transitions. Separate-shard ownership requires a recoverable assignment protocol.', 'Reject late acceptances. On reconnect, fetch durable trip status. Fence stale owners so an expired lock cannot authorize a conflicting write.', '50,000 drivers updating every five seconds create 10,000 location writes/second; coalesce updates and expire old positions.', 'Why might the nearest driver in straight-line distance not have the shortest pickup ETA?'),
('ai-compliance-auditing-rag','AI compliance auditing with RAG','An auditor asks which policy governs storing customer records.', 'Answer from approved, authorized documents with citations; abstain when evidence is missing. Retrieved text is data, not permission to run its instructions.', 'POST /documents for ingestion; POST /questions for retrieval. Document(tenant, version, ACL), Chunk(source/page, text), Embedding, AuditRecord.', ['Auditor','Authorized query API','Filtered retrieval index','Approved document store','Model answer builder','Cited answer + audit'],[(0,1,'question + identity'),(1,2,'tenant / ACL filter'),(2,3,'retrieve source chunks'),(1,4,'authorized evidence only'),(4,5,'answer / abstain')], 'Ingestion extracts approved document versions, chunks them, records provenance and indexes embeddings. At query time enforce tenant/ACL filtering, retrieve evidence, build a constrained prompt, then return citations and an audit record. Ingestion is a separate pipeline from the query arrows.', 'Similarity finds candidates, not truth. Verify citation linkage, document currency and access again as needed. Evaluate retrieval recall separately from answer faithfulness.', 'A deleted or newly restricted document must be removed or filtered in retrieval. Prompt injection in a retrieved chunk must not grant tool authority. Missing evidence should produce abstention.', 'For 10,000 documents × 30 chunks, store 300,000 chunks; estimate vector bytes from dimension × bytes/value plus index overhead.', 'Can a perfectly fluent answer still be wrong if it cites an outdated policy?'),
('video-streaming-platform','Video streaming platform','A teacher uploads a lecture; students watch it on mobile connections.', 'Durable upload, asynchronous transcoding, access-controlled playback and adaptive bitrate quality.', 'POST /uploads issues scoped upload authorization; GET /videos/{id}/playback authorizes a manifest. Video(owner, status), Rendition(codec, bitrate, segments), Job.', ['Uploader','Ingest API / object store','Transcode job queue','Transcode worker','Rendition store + CDN','Viewer player'],[(0,1,'upload source'),(1,2,'after durable upload'),(2,3,'idempotent job'),(3,4,'manifest + segments'),(4,5,'authorized playback')], 'Store the source first and mark processing. Queue a job to produce multiple bitrates and segment manifests. Publish a playable version only when required outputs exist. The player requests segments through the CDN and adapts quality to observed bandwidth.', 'Object storage holds large immutable media; metadata DB tracks ownership/status. Encode asynchronously because it is CPU-heavy and slow relative to an upload acknowledgement.', 'Retries must not publish partial manifests or overwrite a newer version. CDN cache policy and signed/scoped playback access must protect private lectures.', 'At 2 Mbit/second × 10,000 simultaneous viewers, delivery is about 20 Gbit/second before overhead. CDN bandwidth dominates, not metadata QPS.', 'Why should an upload succeed before every video rendition is ready?'),
('notification-platform','Notification platform','A confirmed order triggers an email, an app notification and optionally SMS.', 'Durable intent, preferences, deduplication, retries, scheduling and provider delivery-status tracking.', 'POST /notifications with stable event key. Intent(tenant, recipient, template, channel), Preference, DeliveryAttempt(provider ID/status), DedupKey.', ['Business producer','Notification API','Durable intent + outbox','Channel work queues','Email / SMS workers','External providers'],[(0,1,'event key'),(1,2,'persist accepted intent'),(2,3,'outbox relay'),(3,4,'bounded retries'),(4,5,'provider send')], 'Persist the intent before acknowledging acceptance. Fan out by allowed channel using current preferences and templates. Workers rate-limit providers and record attempts. Delivery callbacks update status; accepted by a provider is not proof that a person read it.', 'A stable event/recipient/channel key prevents duplicate local scheduling. External send needs provider idempotency where available or a documented duplicate-risk policy.', 'Retry transient failures with backoff and jitter; isolate permanent failures for review. Cancellation, unsubscribe and sensitive-message redaction need explicit policies.', 'One million notifications/hour is about 278/second before channel fan-out; a promotion can create a much larger short burst.', 'If the provider accepted an SMS but the acknowledgement was lost, what duplicate risk remains?'),
('distributed-rate-limiter','Distributed rate limiter','Protect a shared API from a tenant sending far more traffic than its plan permits.', 'Enforce per-tenant budgets across API instances with bounded latency and explicit behavior during limiter outages.', 'Middleware check(tenant, route, cost). Policy(capacity, refill_rate), Bucket(tokens, last_refill, expiry). Use bounded key space.', ['Authenticated client','API gateway','Atomic bucket store','Allowed request path','Backend service','429 response path'],[(0,1,'API request'),(1,2,'consume token'),(1,3,'token available'),(3,4,'bounded work'),(1,5,'no token')], 'Compute replenished tokens from elapsed time, capped at capacity, then atomically consume the request cost. Admit only on success. Reject without a token using 429 and useful retry guidance. The allowed and rejection arrows are alternatives, not sequential calls.', 'A capacity-20 bucket refilling 10/second allows a short burst and sustained 10/second. Per-region budgets are simpler but can overshoot a global limit unless coordinated.', 'Decide fail-open versus fail-closed by endpoint risk. Centralized hot keys and datastore outages can turn the limiter into a bottleneck; monitor decision latency.', 'One million requests/minute is about 16,667 limiter decisions/second, so the check must be cheap and measured under contention.', 'Why is GET tokens followed by SET tokens unsafe under concurrent requests?'),
('multi-tenant-saas','Multi-tenant SaaS','Two companies share one project-tracking service but must never see each other’s tasks.', 'Tenant isolation in every data path, per-tenant authorization/quotas, recoverable onboarding and fair resource use.', 'GET /projects derives tenant from authenticated membership. Project primary/unique keys and queries include tenant_id. Tenant, Membership, Project, AuditRecord.', ['Signed-in user','Tenant context + API','Tenant-scoped database','Scoped cache / search','Background workers','Tenant-aware audit'],[(0,1,'verified membership'),(1,2,'tenant-bound query'),(1,3,'tenant-bound keys'),(1,4,'signed job context'),(4,5,'tenant-bound events')], 'Resolve tenant from verified identity and membership. Apply it in database access, cache keys, search filters and object storage authorization. Jobs carry trusted tenant context and recheck permissions as required. Test cross-tenant IDs deliberately, not only happy-path requests.', 'Shared tables are economical but need strict isolation controls; database-per-tenant offers different isolation and operating costs. Large tenants may need separate resource pools.', 'Missing tenant filters leak data. Unscoped cache keys collide. A noisy tenant can consume shared pools, so use per-tenant quotas and workload isolation.', 'Size for both total tenants and largest tenant. One tenant with 80% of requests can invalidate an average-per-tenant capacity estimate.', 'If database queries are scoped correctly but Redis keys omit tenant_id, is isolation complete?'),
('real-time-analytics','Real-time analytics','A dashboard shows recent orders by minute while events arrive late or twice.', 'Durable event ingestion, repeatable aggregates, bounded freshness, late-event handling and tenant-scoped dashboard reads.', 'Event(id, event_time, tenant, order_value); Aggregate(tenant, window, version); GET /metrics?window=. Define event-time versus processing-time semantics.', ['Order event producer','Durable event log','Stream processor','Aggregate store','Dashboard API','Business dashboard'],[(0,1,'stable event IDs'),(1,2,'partitioned events'),(2,3,'window updates'),(3,4,'precomputed metrics'),(4,5,'poll / stream results')], 'Append events durably. A processor groups by event-time window, deduplicates or applies idempotent updates, then writes queryable aggregates. Dashboard reads the aggregate store. Watermarks and an allowed-lateness policy determine when old windows may be revised.', 'Processing time is when an event arrives; event time is when the order happened. Checkpoint/replay semantics must agree with sink idempotency to avoid double-counting.', 'A delayed phone connection can deliver yesterday’s event today. Define correction or side-output policy. Watch consumer lag, backlog and dashboard freshness separately.', 'At 5,000 events/second and 500 bytes/event, raw ingestion is 2.5 MB/second or about 216 GB/day before replicas and indexes.', 'If the processor replays a batch after a crash, how do totals avoid doubling?'),
]

def build():
 root=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
 for slug,title,scenario,requirements,contract,nodes,edges,walk,decisions,failure,capacity,check in CASES:
  esc=html.escape
  xy=[(40,125),(440,125),(840,125),(40,425),(440,425),(840,425)]
  parts=[f'<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="1200" height="650" viewBox="0 0 1200 650" role="img" aria-labelledby="title desc"><title id="title">{esc(title)} architecture</title><desc id="desc">{esc("; ".join(nodes[a]+" to "+nodes[b]+": "+label for a,b,label in edges))}</desc>', '<defs><marker id="arrow" markerWidth="10" markerHeight="10" refX="8" refY="3" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L8,3 L0,6" fill="#38bdf8"/></marker></defs>','<rect width="1200" height="650" rx="18" fill="#0f172a"/>',f'<text x="40" y="48" fill="#f8fafc" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="27" font-weight="bold">{esc(title)} — component view</text>','<text x="40" y="80" fill="#cbd5e1" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="17">Illustrative design • arrows label requests/data; return responses are omitted</text>']
  for a,b,label in edges:
   x,y=xy[a];xx,yy=xy[b]
   if y==yy:
    x1,y1=x+270,y+62;x2,y2=xx,yy+62;tx=(x1+x2)/2;ty=y1-13
   else:
    x1,y1=x+135,y+125;x2,y2=xx+135,yy;tx=(x1+x2)/2;ty=(y1+y2)/2-10
   parts += [f'<path d="M{x1},{y1} L{x2},{y2}" stroke="#38bdf8" stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#arrow)"/>',''.join(f'<text x="{tx}" y="{ty-(len(textwrap.wrap(label,16))-1-j)*18}" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f8fafc" stroke="#0f172a" stroke-width="6" paint-order="stroke" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="14">{esc(line)}</text>' for j,line in enumerate(textwrap.wrap(label,16)))]
  for i,(label,(x,y)) in enumerate(zip(nodes,xy)):
   parts.append(f'<rect x="{x}" y="{y}" width="270" height="125" rx="12" fill="#1e293b" stroke="#7dd3fc"/>')
   for j,line in enumerate(textwrap.wrap(label,24)):
    parts.append(f'<text x="{x+135}" y="{y+55+j*27}" text-anchor="middle" fill="#f1f5f9" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="21">{esc(line)}</text>')
  parts += ['<text x="40" y="610" fill="#fbbf24" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="17">Read the walkthrough for transaction boundaries, alternatives and failure recovery.</text>','</svg>']
  (root/f'docs/system-design/diagrams/{slug}.svg').write_text('\n'.join(parts),encoding='utf-8')
  content=f'''# {title}: a worked design

**Real-world situation:** {scenario}

This is an illustrative architecture, not a claim about a specific company’s implementation.

## What we need and why

{requirements}

## API and data ownership

{contract}

## Component diagram

![{title} architecture](diagrams/{slug}.svg)

Arrows show requests or emitted data. Responses generally return to callers and are omitted. Branches can be alternatives rather than steps that always run.

## Follow one request

{walk}

## Why these components exist

{decisions}

## Failure walkthrough

{failure}

## Capacity worked example

{capacity}

## Explain it back

{check}

[Beginner glossary and learning order](visual-learning-guide.md) · [All case studies](README.md)
'''
  (root/f'docs/system-design/{slug}.md').write_text(content,encoding='utf-8')
 print('Built ten concrete case studies and ten component diagrams.')

if __name__=='__main__':build()
